---
import '../styles/global.css';
import treasuryData from '../../data/treasury-summary.json';
import migrationData from '../../data/migration.json';
import ExitCalculator from '../components/ExitCalculator.astro';
import ObbbaBox from '../components/ObbbaBox.astro';
import StateMap from '../components/StateMap.astro';
---

<html lang="en">
  <!-- deploy-check: 2026-09-04-pat -->
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="Most startups fail. QSBS rewards the founders who built them, the employees who joined early, and the investors who backed them. Here's what the data actually shows." />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://qsbsfacts.org" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="QSBS Facts — Most startups fail. QSBS rewards the ones that don't." />
    <meta property="og:description" content="75% of QSBS claimants use it exactly once. Data-driven analysis of Section 1202 tax policy." />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://qsbsfacts.org" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="QSBS Facts" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://qsbsfacts.org/og-image.png" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="QSBS Facts — Most startups fail. QSBS rewards the ones that don't." />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="75% of QSBS claimants use it exactly once. Data-driven analysis of Section 1202 tax policy." />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://qsbsfacts.org/og-image.png" />
    <title>QSBS Facts — Most startups fail. QSBS rewards the ones that don't.</title>
    <script type="application/ld+json">
    [
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "WebSite",
        "name": "QSBS Facts",
        "url": "https://qsbsfacts.org",
        "description": "Data-driven analysis of Section 1202 Qualified Small Business Stock (QSBS) tax policy."
      },
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "QSBS Facts",
        "url": "https://qsbsfacts.org",
        "description": "Data-driven advocacy for Section 1202 QSBS tax policy based on U.S. Treasury data, academic studies, and migration statistics."
      },
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "WebPage",
        "name": "QSBS Facts — Most startups fail. QSBS rewards the ones that don't.",
        "url": "https://qsbsfacts.org",
        "description": "75% of QSBS claimants use it exactly once. Data-driven analysis of Section 1202 tax policy.",
        "datePublished": "2025-03-20",
        "dateModified": "2026-09-04",
        "citation": [
          {
            "@type": "CreativeWork",
            "name": "U.S. Treasury OTA Working Paper 127",
            "url": "https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/131/WP-127.pdf",
            "datePublished": "2025-01"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
            "name": "Edwards & Todenhaupt (2020)",
            "url": "https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=3520783"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
            "name": "Chen & Farre-Mensa (2023/2025)",
            "url": "https://ssrn.com/abstract=4482626"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What is QSBS (Qualified Small Business Stock)?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "QSBS refers to stock in a qualified small business under IRC Section 1202. Stock acquired on or before July 4, 2025 still needs a 5-year hold and a $10 million per-issuer cap (or 10× basis). Stock acquired after that date, under P.L. 119-21 (OBBBA), uses a 3/4/5-year ladder (50/75/100%) and a $15 million cap, with a $75 million gross-asset test, both indexed after 2026. The exclusion was created in 1993 under President Clinton and expanded to 100% under President Obama in 2010."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Is it true that 94% of QSBS benefits go to millionaires?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "This claim from ITEP is misleading. It's a dollar-weighted statistic, not a count of people. Treasury data (OTA WP-127) shows 74% of QSBS claimants earn under $1 million by average income excluding QSBS gains. ITEP arrives at 94% by adding two groups: 74% of dollars going to people already over $1M, plus 20% going to people earning under $1M whose one-time startup exit temporarily pushed them over. The second group — founders and employees with a single successful exit — is exactly who QSBS was designed for."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Who actually uses QSBS?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "According to U.S. Treasury data (2012–2022): 75% of individuals claimed QSBS in only a single year — one exit, one reward. The median annual exclusion is just $2,810. The 75th percentile is $67,820. The largest claimant group (35%) earns $100K–$400K. These are founders, early employees, and seed investors — not repeat tax avoiders."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Does the QSBS incentive actually work?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Yes. Two peer-reviewed studies provide evidence: Edwards & Todenhaupt (2020) found startup investment increased approximately 12% after the 100% exclusion was enacted in 2010. Chen & Farre-Mensa (2023/2025) found that QSBS-eligible industries experienced more firm births, more startup employment, and increased first-round venture capital compared to ineligible industries."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What happens when states decouple from QSBS?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "States that decouple from federal QSBS tax startup exits that would otherwise be excluded. California never conformed and still hosts the largest U.S. venture cluster — that cluster is path-dependent. The relevant margin for other states is whether they keep founders who can leave. New York considered and rejected QSBS decoupling in its FY2027 budget after founder pushback. Oregon decoupled in April 2026, and Governor Kotek immediately flagged competitiveness and pledged 2027 QSBS legislation. IRS migration data (2021–2022) still shows high-tax states losing filers to Florida and Texas."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
    </script>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700;800&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
  </head>
  <body>

    <!-- Navigation -->
    <nav class="fixed top-0 left-0 right-0 z-50 bg-primary-950/95 backdrop-blur-sm border-b border-primary-800">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 py-3 flex items-center justify-between">
        <a href="#" class="text-white font-bold text-lg">QSBS Facts</a>
        <div class="hidden md:flex items-center gap-6 text-sm text-primary-200">
          <a href="#evidence" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Evidence</a>
          <a href="#who-uses-it" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Who Uses It</a>
          <a href="#claim-vs-reality" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">The 94% Claim</a>
          <a href="#calculator" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Calculator</a>
          <a href="#self-defeating" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Migration</a>
          <a href="#campaign" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Campaign</a>
          <a href="#reform" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Reform</a>
          <a href="#sources" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Sources</a>
        </div>
        <button id="mobile-menu-btn" class="md:hidden text-white" aria-label="Open menu">
          <svg class="w-6 h-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h16"></path></svg>
        </button>
      </div>
      <div id="mobile-menu" class="hidden md:hidden bg-primary-950 border-t border-primary-800 px-4 py-3">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-sm text-primary-200">
          <a href="#evidence" class="hover:text-white">Evidence</a>
          <a href="#who-uses-it" class="hover:text-white">Who Uses It</a>
          <a href="#claim-vs-reality" class="hover:text-white">The 94% Claim</a>
          <a href="#calculator" class="hover:text-white">Calculator</a>
          <a href="#self-defeating" class="hover:text-white">Migration</a>
          <a href="#campaign" class="hover:text-white">Campaign</a>
          <a href="#reform" class="hover:text-white">Reform</a>
          <a href="#sources" class="hover:text-white">Sources</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <!-- 1. Hero -->
    <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-primary-950 via-primary-900 to-primary-800 text-white pt-28 pb-20 px-4 md:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto text-center">
        <p class="text-primary-300 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest mb-4">Section 1202 — Qualified Small Business Stock</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-extrabold leading-tight mb-6">
          Most startups fail.<br/>QSBS rewards the ones that don't.
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-primary-200 max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-4">
          Section 1202 creates an incentive for the founders who build companies, the employees who join early, and the investors who back them at the riskiest stage. 75% of claimants use it exactly once. One bet. One reward.
        </p>
        <p class="text-md text-primary-300 max-w-xl mx-auto mb-10">
          The data tells a different story than the one you've been hearing.
        </p>
        <a href="#evidence" class="inline-block bg-white text-primary-900 font-semibold px-8 py-3 rounded-lg hover:bg-primary-100 transition-colors text-lg">
          See the evidence &darr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. The Evidence -->
    <section id="evidence" class="px-4 py-16 md:px-8 lg:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-center mb-4">The Incentives Are Working</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-center mb-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          Nobody starts a company because of a tax incentive. But QSBS reduces the penalty for taking the risk — and the data shows that matters. When the exclusion was expanded to 100% in 2010, startup investment increased, more companies were created, and more people were employed at startups.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
          {treasuryData.academicStudies.map((study, i) => (
            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 border-l-4 border-primary-500">
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-1">
                {study.ssrn ? (
                  <a href={study.ssrn} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-primary-600 hover:text-primary-800 underline">{study.authors} ({study.year}) ↗</a>
                ) : (
                  <span>{study.authors} ({study.year})</span>
                )}
              </p>
              {i === 0 && (
                <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-primary-700 mb-2">~12%</p>
              )}
              <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">{study.finding}</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500"><strong>Method:</strong> {study.method}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Who Actually Uses It — with archetypes -->
    <section id="who-uses-it" class="px-4 py-16 md:px-8 lg:py-24 bg-gray-50">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-center mb-4">Who Actually Uses It</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-center mb-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          These are the people who took the risk. Founders who spent years building something. Employees who took below-market salary for equity. Investors who wrote checks when nobody else would.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-12">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-primary-700 mb-2">{treasuryData.distribution.oneTimeClaimants.pctSingleYear}%</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600">used it exactly once in 11 years</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-2">One exit. One reward. Only {treasuryData.distribution.oneTimeClaimants.pctFiveOrMoreYears}% claimed it five or more years.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-primary-700 mb-2">$67,820</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600">75th percentile annual exclusion</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-2">The typical meaningful QSBS exit. Median is just ${treasuryData.distribution.medianAnnualExclusion.toLocaleString()}, pulled down by micro-claims.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-primary-700 mb-2">{treasuryData.overview.shareOfAllCapGains2021}%</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600">of all capital gains in 2021</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-2">QSBS is a rounding error in total capital gains.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Archetypes -->
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-center mb-6 text-gray-700">Who QSBS actually helps</h3>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-6 mb-12">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 border-t-4 border-primary-500">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary-600 mb-1">Senior Engineer</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">A senior engineer at a 50-person startup exercises $40K in stock after 6 years.</p>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs">
              <span class="text-gray-400">With QSBS</span>
              <span class="font-bold text-green-600">$0 state tax</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs mt-1">
              <span class="text-gray-400">Without</span>
              <span class="font-bold text-red-600">$5,320</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 border-t-4 border-primary-500">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary-600 mb-1">First-time Founder</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">A first-time founder sells after 8 years for $2M. First liquidity event of their career.</p>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs">
              <span class="text-gray-400">With QSBS</span>
              <span class="font-bold text-green-600">$0 state tax</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs mt-1">
              <span class="text-gray-400">Without</span>
              <span class="font-bold text-red-600">$266,000</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 border-t-4 border-primary-500">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary-600 mb-1">Early Employee</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">Employee #12 exercises options worth $150K after the company is acquired. Five years of below-market salary.</p>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs">
              <span class="text-gray-400">With QSBS</span>
              <span class="font-bold text-green-600">$0 state tax</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs mt-1">
              <span class="text-gray-400">Without</span>
              <span class="font-bold text-red-600">$19,950</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 border-t-4 border-primary-500">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary-600 mb-1">Seed Investor</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">Puts $25K into a friend's company. 7 years later it's worth $200K.</p>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs">
              <span class="text-gray-400">With QSBS</span>
              <span class="font-bold text-green-600">$0 state tax</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex justify-between text-xs mt-1">
              <span class="text-gray-400">Without</span>
              <span class="font-bold text-red-600">$23,275</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 text-center mt-4 mb-8">Tax shown at California's 13.3% rate, the highest in the nation. Your state may differ — <a href="#calculator" class="underline hover:text-gray-600">try the calculator</a>.</p>

        <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6 md:p-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mb-2 text-center">How large is a typical QSBS exclusion?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 text-center mb-4">Annual exclusion amount by percentile. 90% of claimants exclude less than $591K.</p>
          <canvas id="distribution-chart" class="w-full" style="max-height: 300px;"></canvas>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 text-center mt-3">Source: Treasury OTA WP-127, Table 1. Percentiles of annual individual QSBS exclusion claims, 2012-2022.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-center mt-4 text-xs text-gray-400">
          Distribution data: <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/131/WP-127.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline hover:text-gray-600">U.S. Treasury OTA Working Paper 127</a>, January 2025. Archetype examples are illustrative, based on typical QSBS claim profiles.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. The 94% Claim -->
    <section id="claim-vs-reality" class="px-4 py-16 md:px-8 lg:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-center mb-4">The "94% Goes to Millionaires" Claim</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-center mb-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          You've probably heard that 94% of QSBS benefits go to millionaires. The Treasury data shows why that's misleading.
        </p>

        <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-12">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md p-6">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mb-2 text-center">Who claims QSBS?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 text-center mb-4">By number of unique claimants (average income excluding QSBS gains)</p>
            <canvas id="claimants-chart" class="w-full" style="max-height: 300px;"></canvas>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 text-center mt-3">Income measured as average Total Positive Income (TPI), excluding QSBS gains. Source: Treasury OTA WP-127, Figure 4.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-primary-50 rounded-xl p-6 md:p-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          <div class="text-center mb-6">
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-700">Count the <strong>people</strong>, not just the dollars:</p>
            <p class="text-5xl font-bold text-primary-700 mb-2">74%</p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-700">of QSBS claimants earn under $1 million</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">(by average income, excluding QSBS gains)</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2">The single largest group (35%) earns $100K-$400K. These are founders and early employees, not millionaires.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-primary-200 pt-6">
            <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">How ITEP gets to 94%</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-2"><strong>ITEP claims:</strong> "94% of QSBS exclusions were claimed by people with more than $1 million of annual income."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">That's a <em>dollar-weighted</em> stat, not a count of people. Here's how they construct it:</p>
            <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-600">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="shrink-0 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary-200 text-primary-800 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold">1</span>
                <p><strong>{treasuryData.distribution.itepMethodology.pctToOver1M_beforeQSBS}%</strong> of <em>dollars</em> go to people who already earned over $1M before counting QSBS gains</p>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="shrink-0 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-200 text-amber-800 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold">2</span>
                <p><strong>{treasuryData.distribution.itepMethodology.pctToUnder1M_butQSBSpushesOver}%</strong> of <em>dollars</em> go to people earning under $1M normally, whose one-time startup exit temporarily pushed them over</p>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="shrink-0 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-red-200 text-red-800 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold">=</span>
                <p>ITEP <strong>lumps both groups</strong> to claim 94% "goes to millionaires."</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-lg p-4 mt-5">
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-green-800 mb-2">These two groups are the incentive working as designed.</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">The 74% are <strong>investors who funded early-stage companies</strong>. The 20% are <strong>founders and employees who built them</strong>. QSBS connects capital to builders. Both sides show up in the "94%" because both sides took the risk the policy was designed to reward.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-center mt-4 text-xs text-gray-400">
          Source: <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/131/WP-127.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline hover:text-gray-600">U.S. Treasury OTA Working Paper 127</a>, January 2025
        </p>

        <!-- TPI Methodology Explainer -->
        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-6 md:p-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto mt-10">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-amber-900 text-lg mb-3">How Treasury actually measures income (and why it matters)</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-3">
            Treasury's Working Paper 127 classifies QSBS claimants using <strong>Total Positive Income (TPI)</strong>, the sum of positive income sources on a tax return. Critically, <strong>QSBS gains that are excluded from income are also excluded from TPI</strong>. This is a deliberate methodological choice: Treasury measures a claimant's <em>regular economic income</em>, not a one-time liquidity event.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-3">
            Treasury also uses a <strong>3-year average TPI</strong> (the current year plus the prior two years) to classify claimants, smoothing out year-to-year volatility. For unique claimant counts, they average TPI over the full 11-year sample period (2012–2022). This methodology is designed to capture a person's <em>typical</em> income level.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-4 mb-3 border border-amber-100">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 italic">
              <strong>Example:</strong> A founder earning $150K/year sells their company after 8 years for $2M. By Treasury's methodology (which excludes QSBS gains from income), they're classified in the $100K-$400K income bracket. Not a millionaire. ITEP adds the gain back to reach "94%," reclassifying this person as a millionaire based on a single liquidity event.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
            ITEP's approach (adding the excluded QSBS gain back into income) directly undoes Treasury's deliberate choice. The result: people with normal incomes who had one successful exit get lumped in with actual high-income earners, inflating the "millionaire" count.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-3">
            Methodology: <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/131/WP-127.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline hover:text-gray-600">Treasury OTA WP-127</a>, Section III ("Data and Sample Construction")
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Exit Calculator -->
    <section id="calculator" class="px-4 py-16 md:px-8 lg:py-24 bg-gray-50">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-center mb-4">Exit Tax Calculator</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-center mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          See what your state actually takes from a qualified small business stock exit under Section 1202.
        </p>
        <ObbbaBox />
        <ExitCalculator />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6. The Self-Defeating Policy (combines State Map + Migration) -->
    <section id="self-defeating" class="px-4 py-16 md:px-8 lg:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
